  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.select_nodes - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.select_nodes.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.select_nodes()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Select nodes using a predicate function.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.select_nodes()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Select nodes using a predicate function.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.select_nodes()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Select nodes using a predicate function.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nSelect nodes using a predicate function.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L23849-L23980){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.select_nodes(\n    predicate,\n    set_op=\"union\",\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Select nodes by predicate](../assets/graphs/select-nodes.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Six nodes A to F, each with a badge giving its w attribute (3, 8, 6, 2, 9, 4). After selecting with the predicate w greater than 5, nodes B, C, and E, whose values are 8, 6, and 9, are selected and drawn in the accent color.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nCalls a user-supplied function once for every node and selects the nodes for which it\nreturns `True`. This is the most flexible way to build a selection: any rule that can be\nexpressed in Python over a node's ID, label, or attributes can be used.\n\nA node selection is the input to the graph's \"with selection\" (`*_ws`) methods, so a\ntypical workflow is select -> act:\n\n- tag or update a subset of nodes with `set_node_attrs_ws()` or\n  `mutate_node_attrs_ws()`\n- inspect a subset as a table with `get_node_df_ws()`\n- prune nodes with `delete_nodes_ws()` or keep only them with\n  `transform_to_subgraph_ws()`\n- use the nodes as a starting point for `trav_out()`, `trav_in()`, or `trav_both()`\n\nThe matching nodes are added to the current selection, unless `set_op=` says otherwise\n(`\"replace\"`, `\"intersect\"`, or `\"difference\"`). AND/OR logic can also go in the\npredicate itself. The predicate runs in Python for every node, so on very large graphs the\nspecialized selectors (e.g., `select_nodes_by_degree()`) are faster. The graph is\nmodified in place and returned for chaining.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[predicate]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>\n\n:   A callable that receives a `Node` object and returns a `bool`. The `Node` exposes\n    `id` (the integer node ID), `label` (the string label, or `None`), and `attributes`\n    (a dict of the node's attributes, or `None` if the node has no attributes). Nodes for\n    which the callable returns `True` are selected. The return value must be an actual\n    `bool`: returning `None`, an integer, or another truthy object raises `TypeError`.\n    Guard attribute access accordingly, e.g., `n.attributes is not None and ...` or\n    `bool(...)`. Any exception raised by the predicate propagates, and the selection is\n    left as it was.\n\n<code>[set_op]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;union&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   How the matching nodes combine with the current node selection. `\"union\"` (default) adds\n    them to it, `\"replace\"` makes them the selection, `\"intersect\"` keeps only the selected\n    nodes that also match, and `\"difference\"` removes them from it. The graph holds a\n    selection of nodes or of edges, never both, so any edge selection is cleared.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The same graph (modified in place) for method chaining.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [TypeError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the predicate returns something other than a `bool`.\n\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `set_op=` is not `\"union\"`, `\"replace\"`, `\"intersect\"`, or `\"difference\"`.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~select_edges`) \n\n:   Select the edges that pass a test.\n\n[](`~select_match`) \n\n:   Select the nodes or edges that a pattern matches.\n\n[](`~set_node_attrs_ws`) \n\n:   Set attributes on the selected nodes.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe predicate gets each node as a `Node` object, so the rule can look at its `id`, `label`\nor `attributes`. Here it picks the nodes whose `kind` is `\"hub\"`:\n\n::: {#0f8d766e .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = (\n    ec.Graph()\n    .add_node(label=\"A\", kind=\"hub\", score=10)\n    .add_node(label=\"B\", kind=\"leaf\", score=5)\n    .add_node(label=\"C\", kind=\"hub\", score=8)\n    .select_nodes(lambda n: n.attributes[\"kind\"] == \"hub\")\n)\n\nlabel = graph.get_label_from_id\nsorted(label(n) for n in graph.get_selected_node_ids())\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['A', 'C']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nWith `set_op=\"intersect\"`, a second rule narrows the selection. Of the two hubs, only `A`\nhas a score above 9:\n\n::: {#7e6c20bc .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph.select_nodes(\n    lambda n: n.attributes[\"score\"] > 9, set_op=\"intersect\"\n)\n[label(n) for n in graph.get_selected_node_ids()]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n['A']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe predicate can't call methods of the graph itself, since the graph is in use while\nit runs. Work out what it needs beforehand, as with the degrees here:\n\n::: {#1058d849 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\")])\ndegree = {n: graph.get_degree(n) for n in graph.get_node_ids()}\n\ngraph.select_nodes(lambda n: degree[n.id] == 1)\nsorted(graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in graph.get_selected_node_ids())\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n['B', 'D']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### The Ring line\n\n`metro()` is a made-up city's metro network, and each station's `lines` attribute lists\nthe lines that stop there. Six stations are on the Ring line, and four of them are\ninterchanges, where a rider can change to another line:\n\n::: {#817b9a59 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro = ec.metro()\nmetro.select_nodes(lambda n: \"Ring\" in n.attributes[\"lines\"])\nring = metro.count_selected_nodes()\n\nmetro.select_nodes(\n    lambda n: len(n.attributes[\"lines\"]) > 1, set_op=\"intersect\"\n)\nlabel = metro.get_label_from_id\nring, sorted(label(n) for n in metro.get_selected_node_ids())\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n(6, ['Market', 'Museum', 'Old Town', 'Riverside'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#b3d49b6f .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro.plot(layout=\"pos\", highlight=\"selection\", label_by=\"label\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xm